{
  "concept": "The enthalpy change for sublimation equals the sum of the enthalpies of fusion and vaporization, because enthalpy depends only on the initial and final states, not on the path taken.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Black vertical arrow along the left edge pointing up, bold label 'Energy' rotated vertically beside it",
    "Horizontal blue line near the bottom labeled 'Solid' at its right end",
    "Horizontal blue line about one third of the way up labeled 'Liquid' at its right end",
    "Horizontal blue line near the top labeled 'Gas' at its right end (Solid-Liquid gap noticeably smaller than Liquid-Gas gap)",
    "Thick red upward arrow on the left spanning Solid line to Gas line (sublimation)",
    "Text label 'Sublimation' with 'ΔH_sub' beneath it, placed to the left of the arrow pair in the Solid-to-Liquid band",
    "Thick red upward arrow, right column, spanning Solid line to Liquid line (fusion)",
    "Text label 'Fusion' with 'ΔH_fus' beneath it, to the right of the fusion arrow",
    "Thick red upward arrow, right column, spanning Liquid line to Gas line (vaporization)",
    "Text label 'Vaporization' with 'ΔH_vap' beneath it, to the right of the vaporization arrow",
    "Running-total energy marker: a horizontal tick / filled bar on the far right that rises from the Solid level to the current state level during the stepped sequence",
    "Summation annotation that appears when both step arrows are complete: 'ΔH_sub = ΔH_fus + ΔH_vap'",
    "Faint horizontal dashed guide line connecting the top of the sublimation arrow to the top of the vaporization arrow at the Gas level"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "phasePath",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step the phase path",
      "teaches": "Melting and then boiling carries the sample from the Solid level to the Gas level in two additive energy steps, and the running total ends at exactly the Gas line.",
      "states": ["solid", "melted", "vaporized"],
      "default": "solid"
    },
    {
      "id": "pathView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Route from solid to gas",
      "teaches": "The same initial and final states can be reached directly or in two steps, and the total vertical rise — the enthalpy change — is identical either way.",
      "options": ["Direct: sublimation", "Two-step: fusion + vaporization", "Compare both"],
      "default": "Compare both"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Three energy levels",
      "narration": "Three blue lines mark the relative enthalpy of solid, liquid and gas for the same substance. Higher line means higher energy; the liquid-to-gas gap is much larger than the solid-to-liquid gap.",
      "state": { "pathView": "Compare both", "phasePath": "solid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The direct route: sublimation",
      "narration": "One long red arrow takes the solid straight to the gas. Its length is ΔH_sub, the energy that must be absorbed for sublimation.",
      "state": { "pathView": "Direct: sublimation", "phasePath": "solid" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 1 — melting",
      "narration": "Switch to the two-step route and add ΔH_fus: the sample climbs only from the Solid line to the Liquid line, and the running total on the right rises by that short amount.",
      "state": { "pathView": "Two-step: fusion + vaporization", "phasePath": "melted" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step 2 — vaporizing",
      "narration": "Adding ΔH_vap lifts the liquid the rest of the way to the Gas line. The running total now sits exactly at the top of the sublimation arrow.",
      "state": { "pathView": "Two-step: fusion + vaporization", "phasePath": "vaporized" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Both routes, same height",
      "narration": "Show the long arrow beside the two stacked arrows: they end at the same level, so ΔH_sub = ΔH_fus + ΔH_vap. Enthalpy is a state function — the path does not matter.",
      "state": { "pathView": "Compare both", "phasePath": "vaporized" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Two stacked arrows reach exactly the same height as the single long arrow, so the enthalpy of sublimation is just melting plus boiling added together — enthalpy depends only on the start and end states.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "White background, landscape layout. Left edge: a thin black arrow pointing up with the bold vertical word 'Energy'; no numbers or tick marks anywhere. Three thick horizontal royal-blue lines span most of the width: 'Solid' low, 'Liquid' at roughly one third height, 'Gas' near the top, each label in plain black text just outside the right end of its line (Gas and Liquid above their lines, Solid below). The Liquid-to-Gas span should be about twice the Solid-to-Liquid span. Two vertical columns of thick dark-red (firebrick) arrows with solid triangular heads sit in the middle-left area: the left column is one arrow from Solid to Gas labeled 'Sublimation / ΔH_sub' (text to its left, in the lower band); the right column is a short arrow Solid→Liquid labeled 'Fusion / ΔH_fus' to its right in the lower band, plus an arrow Liquid→Gas labeled 'Vaporization / ΔH_vap' to its right in the upper band. Inactive arrows in a view should fade to light gray rather than disappear, so the comparison stays legible. During the stepped sequence, highlight the active arrow (brighter red, slight glow) and grow a slim vertical running-total bar along the far right from the Solid level up to the current state, capped by a small horizontal tick; when it reaches Gas, draw a dashed gray horizontal line across to the top of the sublimation arrow and show the annotation 'ΔH_sub = ΔH_fus + ΔH_vap' in the upper-left whitespace."
}

